course: everyday
lesson: 213
title: Sadness, jealousy, boredom, pride and brothers and sisters
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Every feeling tells us something, and each has a good thing to do with it.
explain: Sadness that lasts many days and takes away the fun of things you loved can be an illness called depression, and it can be helped, so you tell a parent, teacher or doctor.
explain: Jealousy, loneliness and guilt fade when you act: you ask for time, join a group, or say sorry and make up for it.
explain: A girl in Cardiff is lonely after moving house; she joins the library club and says hello to the girl next door, and soon has two new friends.
explain: Boredom is the start of creativity: a rainy day with no screen can become a den, a story or a cake.
fact: Feeling sad for many days and losing interest in things you loved can be an illness called depression.
fact: Long sadness can be helped, so you tell a parent, teacher or doctor.
fact: If you feel jealous of a new baby, you can ask a parent for special time with just you; parents love you just as much.
fact: Babies simply need a lot of care.
fact: On a bored rainy day you can build a den, read, draw, write a story, do a puzzle or help cook.
fact: If a lesson is too easy, you can politely ask the teacher for a harder challenge, because teachers often have extension work.
fact: When you are excited, you wait for a pause before you speak, because people feel respected when they can finish.
fact: Boasting makes others feel small and pushes friends away, so you share the fun instead.
fact: In a new town, a club, team or library group and saying hello to neighbours your age help you make friends.
fact: Guilt shows you care; saying sorry and doing something kind helps you let it go.
fact: You do not laugh when a brother or sister gets into trouble, because you would want kindness in your turn.
fact: In a family where a brother or sister has a disability or illness, you ask questions, help where you can and tell your parents if you feel left out.
fact: Older brothers and sisters need time with friends their age, so you find your own fun and do something together another time.
exercise: For weeks you have felt sad and no longer enjoy football, which you loved. What do you do? | Tell a parent, teacher or doctor | Keep it to yourself until it passes | Stay in your bedroom more and more | Pretend to be happy all of the time
exercise: Why should long sadness be talked about? | It can be an illness called depression, and it can be helped | Sadness always goes away in one day | Talking about sadness makes it worse | Only adults get sad for long
exercise: Your parents spend all their time with the new baby. What can you do? | Ask a parent for special time with just you | Hide the baby's toys so they have to look for them | Stop talking to your parents | Act like a baby so they notice you
exercise: Why does a new baby get so much attention? | Babies simply need a lot of care | Parents always love the newest baby a lot more | You must have done something wrong | The baby asked for the attention itself
exercise: It is raining and the screens are off. What could you do? | Build a den, read, draw or help cook | Complain until the screens come back | Sit and stare at the wall all day | Go to bed at lunchtime
exercise: You find the maths lesson too easy and are bored. What is best? | Politely ask the teacher for a harder challenge | Chat to your neighbour instead | Tell everyone loudly that the lesson is far too babyish | Pretend to be ill to go home
exercise: You are so excited about your news that you keep interrupting. What should you do? | Wait for a pause before you speak | Talk louder than everyone else there | Tell your news while the others are talking | Walk away if nobody listens at once
exercise: You have a lot of new toys. A friend has few. What is kind? | Share the fun instead of boasting | Tell everyone exactly how many you have | Show off the price of each one of them | Play with them only in front of others
exercise: You have moved to a new town and feel lonely. What helps? | Join a club, team or library group and say hello to neighbours | Stay inside until someone knocks | Wait for school to finish so you can be alone | Message only your old friends all day
exercise: You were mean to a classmate and now feel bad. What do you do? | Say sorry and do something kind to make up for it | Forget about it and hope that she forgets it too by Monday | Be mean again so it feels normal | Blame someone else
exercise: What does guilt show about you? | That you care | That you are a bad person forever | That you are ill | That the other person was wrong
exercise: Your brother is told off by Dad. You feel like laughing. What do you do? | Do not laugh or tease | Laugh, since it was funny | Tell all your friends at school | Join in telling him off
exercise: Your sister has a long illness and gets a lot of attention. You feel left out. What is best? | Tell your parents how you feel, ask questions and help where you can | Keep it inside and be cross with her | Pretend to be ill too | Stop talking to everyone
exercise: Your older brother will not let you play with his friends. What is fair? | Find your own fun and do something with him another time | Follow them everywhere anyway | Tell on him to your parents | Hide his phone
test: A boy in Seattle has stopped seeing friends and enjoying anything for a month. What is true? | He should tell an adult, because long sadness can be helped | He is just lazy | It will surely pass if he hides it | Children never get depression
test: A girl says, "Mum loves the new baby more than me." What is true? | Parents still love her as much; babies just need lots of care | Parents can love only one child at a time, so the newest one wins | She must earn back the love | The baby will always get more love
test: A boy in Wellington says rainy days are useless without a tablet. What is a good idea? | Use the time to build, read, draw or cook something | Sleep until the rain stops | Complain until it is sunny | Stare out the window all day
test: A girl finishes all her work in five minutes every lesson. What could she politely do? | Ask the teacher for extension work | Sit quietly doing nothing all year long | Tell the class the work is too easy | Do other pupils' work for them quietly
test: At lunch, two friends are telling a story and you burst in with yours. How do they likely feel? | Not respected, because they could not finish | Grateful for the new story, since theirs was going on | Happy to stop talking | Nothing at all
test: A boy keeps saying his house is bigger than everyone's. What is likely to happen? | Friends feel small and move away from him | Everyone wants to be his friend more, so they can visit the big house | He becomes more popular | Nothing changes
test: A girl pushed a younger child in the queue and feels guilty. What will help her feel better? | Saying sorry and doing something kind for the child | Avoiding the child forever, so neither of them remembers it | Pushing someone else | Telling herself it did not happen
test: Your older sister's friends are over and she wants time with them. What is kind? | Let them be and plan something with your sister later | Sit right in the middle of the group so you are part of it | Play loud music outside her door | Tell her friends embarrassing stories
test: Your brother is in trouble for breaking a vase. Why not tease him? | You would want kindness when it is your turn | Teasing him somehow helps to fix the broken vase | Parents like it when you tease | He deserves to feel worse
test: been sad for weeks, dont even like football anymore | Tell a parent, teacher or doctor; long sadness can be helped | Keep it secret, it will pass | Just play more football | It's normal, ignore it
test: new baby gets all the attention, mum loves her more | Ask for special time with just you; parents love you just as much | It's true, babies are loved more | Be mean to the baby | Hide until they notice
test: maths is too easy, so boring | Politely ask the teacher for a harder challenge | Mess around in class till it's over | Just stop doing the work, they can't make you | Tell everyone it's too easy
test: my bro got told off lol | Don't laugh, you'd want kindness in your turn | Laugh loudly at him so he knows he messed up | Tell everyone at school | Make it worse
explain: A boy in New York felt sad for months after his best friend moved away but told nobody; he stopped playing football and eating well, until his teacher noticed and got him help.
explain: A girl in Perth was jealous of her sister's new bike and let the tyres down; her sister rode it without checking, fell and grazed both knees.
fact: Sadness that lasts for weeks and stops you enjoying things is worth telling a trusted adult about, because help works.
fact: Acting on jealousy by spoiling someone's things can get them hurt and can break trust for a long time.
test: been sad for ages, don't wanna do anything | Tell a parent or teacher how you feel | Keep it in, it'll pass on its own soon enough | Stay in your room more | Pretend you're fine
test: Why was letting down the bike tyres a dangerous thing to do? | The rider could fall and get hurt | It only wastes some of the air inside | Flat tyres make the whole bike dirty | Flat tyres can never be pumped again
test: so bored i'm gonna climb the garage roof | No, find something safe, roofs are a bad fall | Yes, boredom's the worst, anything's better than that | Yes, if nobody's watching | Only the low side
test: did u hear that girl's been crying every day for weeks and doesn't even go to dance anymore, everyone thinks she's just being dramatic. real lesson? | Long sadness can be an illness; an adult can help | She's just being dramatic for attention, so ignore her | It'll pass on its own in a day | Sadness is only a problem for grown-ups
test: apparently the new kid boasts he's got 3 iPads and a pool and nobody likes him now. what's the true lesson behind it? | Boasting pushes friends away; share the fun instead | Boasting makes people like you more, if your stuff is cool | Kids with pools always have more friends | Never tell anyone anything good about you
test: everyone's saying that boy's mum loves his baby sister more, he said so himself. pass it on? | No, it's private and parents love both just as much | Yes, it must be true if he said it himself, so it's fine | Yes, so his mum finds out | Yes, it's not a secret anyway
exam: Identify the condition which may be characterised as the illness called depression. | Sadness lasting many days with loss of interest in things once loved | Sadness lasting a few minutes after a defeat | Anger felt upon losing a game | Boredom on a single rainy day
exam: Which of the following constitutes an appropriate request where a lesson is too easy for a pupil? | A polite request to the teacher for a harder challenge | A complaint to the whole class that the lesson is dull and childish | The refusal to complete the work set | The completion of other pupils' work
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning jealousy upon the arrival of a new baby. | The child may ask for special time; parents' love is undiminished | Parents cease to love older children | Jealousy of a baby is never felt | The child ought to keep the feeling hidden
exam: Ascertain the effect of boasting upon friendships. | It causes others to feel small and drives friends away | It invariably increases one's friends and one's standing among them | It has no effect upon others | It causes others to feel proud
